select c.id, $L$m1-15$L$, $L$Fundamentals of Testing$L$, 1, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$Why is traceability between the test basis and test work products valuable?$L$, $J$[{"text":"It supports impact analysis and coverage measurement, so teams can see which requirements are tested and what a change affects","correct":true,"why":"Traceability links requirements to tests and results, enabling coverage evaluation and assessment of the impact of changes."},{"text":"It automatically fixes defects that are linked to requirements","correct":false,"why":"Traceability provides visibility and links; it does not perform fixes."},{"text":"It removes the need to write test cases because links replace them","correct":false,"why":"Traceability connects test cases to the basis; it does not replace the cases themselves."},{"text":"It guarantees that every requirement is defect-free once linked","correct":false,"why":"Linking a requirement to a test does not guarantee the requirement or the test is free of defects."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Fundamentals of Testing highlights traceability between the test basis, test conditions, test cases, and results as a way to measure coverage, assess the impact of changes, and support audits and reporting. It makes testing transparent and controllable.$L$, $L$When a regulator asks which tests cover a new anti-fraud requirement, the team follows traceability links from the requirement to its test cases and their latest pass/fail results.$L$
