select c.id, $L$m1-17$L$, $L$Fundamentals of Testing$L$, 1, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$Why is a degree of independence of testing generally considered beneficial?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Independent testers are more likely to recognize different kinds of failures due to differing assumptions and cognitive biases","correct":true,"why":"Independence brings a fresh perspective and reduces author bias, so independent testers often find defects the author overlooked."},{"text":"Independent testers can approve their own code faster","correct":false,"why":"Independence is about separation from the author, not self-approval, which would reintroduce bias."},{"text":"Independence removes the need for developers to test at all","correct":false,"why":"Developers still test their own work; independence complements rather than replaces that."},{"text":"Independence guarantees that all defects will be found","correct":false,"why":"No level of independence guarantees finding all defects; it improves the chance of finding some, not all."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$The psychology of testing section explains that independence increases the effectiveness of finding defects because independent testers hold different assumptions than the authors and are less subject to author bias. Independence can range from developers testing their own code to a separate external test organization.$L$, $L$A separate QA team testing a checkout flow spots an edge case the original developer assumed could never happen, illustrating the value of independent perspective.$L$
