select c.id, $L$m1-18$L$, $L$Fundamentals of Testing$L$, 1, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$A tester must report a serious defect to a developer who is proud of the feature. Which approach best reflects good testing psychology?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Communicate findings objectively and factually, focusing on the product and shared goals rather than blaming the person","correct":true,"why":"Constructive, fact-based communication that focuses on the work product preserves collaboration and keeps the whole team aligned on quality."},{"text":"Emphasize how many mistakes the developer made to make the severity clear","correct":false,"why":"Blaming individuals damages relationships and undermines the collaborative goal of improving the product."},{"text":"Avoid reporting the defect to keep the peace","correct":false,"why":"Withholding defect information conflicts with the tester's objective of providing accurate quality information."},{"text":"Report the defect only to management to bypass the developer","correct":false,"why":"Bypassing the developer erodes trust and hinders the whole-team approach; direct, respectful communication is preferred."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$The psychology of testing emphasizes communicating defects constructively: stay objective, focus on the product and facts, and frame findings around shared quality goals to avoid triggering defensiveness. This supports collaboration between testers and the rest of the team.$L$, $L$A tester tells a developer that the funds-transfer screen accepts negative amounts, showing the reproduction steps and the requirement, rather than criticizing the developer's competence.$L$
