select c.id, $L$m1-19$L$, $L$Fundamentals of Testing$L$, 1, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$What does the whole-team approach to quality imply?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Everyone on the team shares responsibility for quality, with testers and developers collaborating throughout","correct":true,"why":"The whole-team approach means quality is a collective responsibility, with team members bringing their skills to testing tasks together."},{"text":"Only the dedicated test team is responsible for quality","correct":false,"why":"This contradicts the whole-team idea, which spreads responsibility across all roles."},{"text":"Developers should never participate in any testing activity","correct":false,"why":"In a whole-team approach developers actively contribute to testing, for example through unit tests and pairing."},{"text":"Quality is guaranteed simply because the whole team is involved","correct":false,"why":"Shared responsibility improves quality but does not guarantee a defect-free product."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Fundamentals of Testing describes the whole-team approach, common in Agile, where any team member with the necessary knowledge participates in testing and quality is a shared responsibility. Collaboration between testers, developers, and business representatives strengthens outcomes.$L$, $L$On an insurance-quote feature, developers write unit tests, testers design end-to-end scenarios, and the product owner reviews acceptance criteria, all sharing responsibility for quality.$L$
