select c.id, $L$m1-21$L$, $L$Fundamentals of Testing$L$, 1, $L$K3$L$, null, null, $L$A team has limited time to test a large payments release. Given the defect clustering and risk-based principles, which strategy is most appropriate?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Focus deeper testing on the historically defect-prone and high-risk transaction modules first","correct":true,"why":"Defect clustering and risk-based prioritization direct effort to areas most likely to fail and most costly if they do, maximizing value under time constraints."},{"text":"Distribute the same shallow test effort evenly across every module regardless of risk","correct":false,"why":"Uniform effort ignores clustering and risk, wasting time on low-risk areas while under-testing critical ones."},{"text":"Attempt exhaustive testing of all input combinations to be safe","correct":false,"why":"Exhaustive testing is impossible and infeasible in limited time, so it is not a viable strategy."},{"text":"Skip testing and rely solely on the developers' confidence in their code","correct":false,"why":"Author confidence is subject to bias and does not substitute for independent, risk-focused testing."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Applying the defect clustering principle and risk-based prioritization, testers concentrate effort where failures are most probable and most impactful. This is a practical application of the principles that exhaustive testing is impossible and that testing is context dependent.$L$, $L$With two days before a payments release, the lead schedules intensive testing of the settlement and refund modules that caused past incidents, and only smoke tests for stable areas.$L$
