select c.id, $L$m1-22$L$, $L$Fundamentals of Testing$L$, 1, $L$K3$L$, null, null, $L$A new requirement is added late to a project. Using traceability, how should the test team respond most effectively?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Trace the requirement to identify new test conditions and check which existing tests are affected, then update coverage","correct":true,"why":"Traceability enables impact analysis: linking the new requirement to conditions and existing tests shows exactly what must be added or revised."},{"text":"Rerun the entire old test suite unchanged and assume it covers the new requirement","correct":false,"why":"The unchanged suite predates the requirement, so it cannot cover it; this misses new conditions."},{"text":"Ignore the requirement because tests already exist for the feature area","correct":false,"why":"Existing tests do not necessarily cover the new behavior; ignoring it leaves a coverage gap."},{"text":"Delete all existing tests and start the test basis from scratch","correct":false,"why":"Discarding valid tests wastes effort; traceability lets you extend coverage without starting over."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Traceability between the test basis and test work products supports impact analysis when requirements change. The team follows links to determine which new test conditions are needed and which existing tests require updates, keeping coverage accurate and efficient.$L$, $L$When a new strong-authentication rule is added to a banking app, testers trace it to affected login tests, add multi-factor scenarios, and update the coverage matrix.$L$
