select c.id, $L$m1-23$L$, $L$Fundamentals of Testing$L$, 1, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$Which statement about verification and validation is correct?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Verification checks whether the product is built according to specifications, while validation checks whether it meets user needs","correct":true,"why":"Verification confirms conformance to specified requirements; validation confirms the product satisfies the actual needs of users and stakeholders."},{"text":"Verification and validation both only occur after release","correct":false,"why":"Both can occur throughout the lifecycle, including early static checks, not only after release."},{"text":"Validation checks conformance to the specification and verification checks user needs","correct":false,"why":"This reverses the definitions of the two terms."},{"text":"Verification and validation mean exactly the same thing","correct":false,"why":"They are related but distinct objectives, one focused on specifications and one on user needs."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Among the typical test objectives, the syllabus distinguishes verification (are we building the product right, per the specification) from validation (are we building the right product, per user needs). Both contribute to confidence in the product.$L$, $L$A team verifies that a search API returns results in the specified JSON format, and validates that the search actually helps shoppers find the products they want.$L$
