select c.id, $L$m1-24$L$, $L$Fundamentals of Testing$L$, 1, $L$K1$L$, null, null, $L$Which of the following is an example of testing being context dependent?$L$, $J$[{"text":"A safety-critical medical device is tested far more rigorously than an internal prototype dashboard","correct":true,"why":"Adjusting rigor and approach based on the domain and risk is exactly what the context-dependent principle describes."},{"text":"Every project uses the identical fixed set of test cases regardless of domain","correct":false,"why":"Using identical tests everywhere ignores context, contradicting the principle."},{"text":"Testing always requires the same amount of time on every project","correct":false,"why":"Fixed time regardless of context contradicts context dependence."},{"text":"The same tester must always perform every test on all projects","correct":false,"why":"Staffing consistency is unrelated to and does not illustrate context dependence."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$The principle testing is context dependent states that testing is carried out differently in different contexts, driven by factors such as risk level, domain, regulations, and development approach. Higher-risk contexts warrant more thorough testing.$L$, $L$The same company tests a pacemaker firmware release with formal, documented, exhaustive risk analysis, but tests an internal marketing dashboard with quick exploratory checks.$L$
