select c.id, $L$m1-25$L$, $L$Fundamentals of Testing$L$, 1, $L$K1$L$, null, null, $L$Which statement correctly distinguishes a defect from its root cause?$L$, $J$[{"text":"A defect is a flaw in a work product, while a root cause is the underlying reason the defect was introduced","correct":true,"why":"The syllabus defines the root cause as the fundamental reason for a problem, which is deeper than the individual defect it produced."},{"text":"A defect and a root cause are two names for the same failure","correct":false,"why":"A defect is a specific flaw; the root cause explains why that and often other defects arose, so they are not the same."},{"text":"A root cause is always a single line of incorrect code","correct":false,"why":"A root cause is frequently a process or knowledge gap, not merely one line of code."},{"text":"A defect is the human mistake and a root cause is the observable failure","correct":false,"why":"The human mistake is an error and the observable behavior is a failure; this option mislabels both terms."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Fundamentals of Testing distinguishes defects from their root causes: a root cause is the fundamental reason a defect was introduced, and analyzing it can prevent whole classes of similar defects. Root cause analysis targets process improvement, not just fixing the single defect.$L$, $L$Several checkout defects trace to one root cause: developers lacked a shared definition of how to handle currency rounding, so fixing the standard prevents future rounding defects.$L$
