select c.id, $L$m2-02$L$, $L$Software Development Lifecycle$L$, 2, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$The V-model is often preferred over classic Waterfall for testing because it does which of the following?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Pairs each development level with a corresponding test level, encouraging early test design","correct":true,"why":"The V-model maps each specification or design activity to a matching test level, promoting early verification and validation."},{"text":"Removes the need for a separate acceptance test level","correct":false,"why":"The V-model retains acceptance testing; it does not remove any test level."},{"text":"Guarantees that no defects reach production","correct":false,"why":"No model can guarantee zero defects; testing reduces risk, it does not eliminate it."},{"text":"Replaces all dynamic testing with static reviews","correct":false,"why":"The V-model uses both static and dynamic testing; it does not replace one with the other."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$The V-model extends the sequential approach by associating each development phase with a corresponding test level, so test design starts as the matching specification is written. This early involvement is a key testing advantage of the V-model within sequential SDLCs.$L$, $L$On a medical-device project, testers draft system test cases from the system requirements while developers are still coding, so tests are ready the moment a build arrives.$L$
