select c.id, $L$m2-06$L$, $L$Software Development Lifecycle$L$, 2, $L$K1$L$, null, null, $L$Which good testing practice states that testers should be involved in requirement and design reviews as early as possible?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Early testing, often described as shift-left","correct":true,"why":"Shift-left promotes involving testing activities, including reviews, as early as feasible in the lifecycle."},{"text":"Late testing to conserve effort","correct":false,"why":"Delaying testing raises the cost of fixing defects; it is not a good practice."},{"text":"Testing only after user acceptance","correct":false,"why":"Acceptance is the last level; restricting testing to it ignores earlier defect detection."},{"text":"Avoiding tester involvement in reviews","correct":false,"why":"Testers add value in reviews by spotting testability and ambiguity issues early."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$The shift-left approach embodies the early-testing principle by involving testers in reviews of requirements and designs before code exists. Finding defects early through static testing is cheaper than finding them during later dynamic execution.$L$, $L$A tester joining sprint refinement flags an ambiguous acceptance criterion for a login timeout, preventing a costly misunderstanding before any code is written.$L$
