select c.id, $L$m2-10$L$, $L$Software Development Lifecycle$L$, 2, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$In a DevOps environment, what is a primary benefit of continuous integration for testing?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Fast, automated feedback on code quality because tests run on every integration","correct":true,"why":"CI triggers automated builds and tests on each change, giving rapid feedback and catching defects early."},{"text":"It removes the need for any automated tests","correct":false,"why":"DevOps relies heavily on automated testing; it increases, not removes, that need."},{"text":"It defers all testing until a manual release gate","correct":false,"why":"DevOps aims to test continuously, not defer testing to a single late gate."},{"text":"It eliminates the need for version control","correct":false,"why":"Continuous integration depends on version control; it does not eliminate it."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$DevOps and continuous integration provide rapid automated feedback by building and testing on every change, so defects are surfaced quickly. This continuous, automated testing is central to how DevOps shapes testing activities in the SDLC.$L$, $L$Each time a developer pushes to the shared branch, the CI pipeline runs unit and integration tests within minutes and blocks the merge if any fail.$L$
