select c.id, $L$m2-12$L$, $L$Software Development Lifecycle$L$, 2, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$What is the main idea behind the shift-left approach to testing?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Perform testing activities earlier in the lifecycle to find defects sooner","correct":true,"why":"Shift-left moves testing tasks earlier so defects are detected and corrected when they are cheaper to fix."},{"text":"Move all testing to the final release stage","correct":false,"why":"That is shift-right or late testing, the opposite of shift-left."},{"text":"Assign testing exclusively to the operations team","correct":false,"why":"Shift-left is about timing, not about reassigning testing to operations."},{"text":"Test only the left-hand modules of the architecture","correct":false,"why":"Left here refers to earlier in time on the timeline, not a physical part of the system."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Shift-left means starting testing activities earlier in the SDLC, including static testing and early test design, to catch defects when correction is least expensive. It is a widely recommended good practice across modern lifecycles.$L$, $L$A team introduces static code analysis and requirement reviews in the first sprint, catching a null-handling flaw during design rather than in a later system test.$L$
