select c.id, $L$m2-13$L$, $L$Software Development Lifecycle$L$, 2, $L$K1$L$, null, null, $L$What is the purpose of a retrospective in an iterative or Agile SDLC?$L$, $J$[{"text":"To reflect on what went well and what to improve, feeding process improvement","correct":true,"why":"Retrospectives are structured reflection points used to identify improvements to process, tools, and collaboration."},{"text":"To assign blame for defects found in the last release","correct":false,"why":"Retrospectives are blameless improvement sessions, not blame assignment."},{"text":"To write the detailed test cases for the next release","correct":false,"why":"Test-case writing is a separate activity; retrospectives focus on process improvement."},{"text":"To approve the production deployment","correct":false,"why":"Deployment approval is a release decision, not the purpose of a retrospective."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Retrospectives give the team a regular opportunity to review the completed iteration and agree improvements, which can strengthen test effectiveness and efficiency. They are a common good practice that supports continuous improvement across the SDLC.$L$, $L$In a sprint retrospective the team notices flaky automated tests wasted time, and they agree to stabilize the test environment before the next sprint.$L$
