select c.id, $L$m2-17$L$, $L$Software Development Lifecycle$L$, 2, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$Who most commonly performs acceptance testing, and what is its main objective?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Customers or users, to establish confidence that the system is fit for purpose and ready for use","correct":true,"why":"Acceptance testing validates readiness and fitness for use, typically from the perspective of users or customers."},{"text":"Developers, to check the internal logic of each function","correct":false,"why":"Internal-logic checking by developers is component testing, not acceptance testing."},{"text":"Testers, to expose interface defects between modules","correct":false,"why":"Interface defect detection is the aim of integration testing."},{"text":"Operations staff, to measure code coverage percentages","correct":false,"why":"Coverage measurement is a technique used earlier; it is not the objective of acceptance testing."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Acceptance testing focuses on building confidence that the system is fit for purpose and ready for deployment, and is often performed by or on behalf of customers and users. Its objective and typical actors distinguish it from earlier test levels.$L$, $L$Before rolling out a new HR portal, a group of HR staff run user acceptance tests against real business processes to confirm the system supports their daily work.$L$
