select c.id, $L$m2-23$L$, $L$Software Development Lifecycle$L$, 2, $L$K3$L$, null, null, $L$A team fixes one defect late in a release with very limited time before deployment. Which combination of activities best manages the risk introduced by the change?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Confirmation testing of the fix plus a risk-based selection of regression tests around the affected areas","correct":true,"why":"You confirm the fix works and then run targeted regression tests where the change is most likely to have side effects, given time limits."},{"text":"Confirmation testing only, skipping any regression testing","correct":false,"why":"Skipping regression ignores the risk that the change broke other functionality."},{"text":"Re-running the entire regression suite manually with no confirmation test","correct":false,"why":"Without confirmation you have not verified the fix, and a full manual rerun is impractical in limited time."},{"text":"No testing, because only one line changed","correct":false,"why":"Even small changes can cause regressions; testing the change is still required."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Confirmation testing verifies the specific fix, while regression testing detects unintended side effects; under time pressure a risk-based selection of regression tests focuses effort where side effects are most likely. Applying both, prioritized by risk, is the recommended response to a late change.$L$, $L$With one day before release, a tester re-runs the failed order-total test to confirm the fix, then runs the automated regression tests covering orders, payments, and invoicing rather than the entire suite.$L$
