select c.id, $L$m3-02$L$, $L$Static Testing$L$, 3, $L$K1$L$, null, null, $L$Which work product CANNOT normally be examined by static testing?$L$, $J$[{"text":"The runtime memory-leak behavior of a running application","correct":true,"why":"Runtime behavior only appears while the software executes, so it requires dynamic testing."},{"text":"A requirements specification document","correct":false,"why":"Requirements are a classic candidate for static review."},{"text":"Source code and its comments","correct":false,"why":"Source code can be reviewed and statically analyzed without execution."},{"text":"Test cases and test scripts","correct":false,"why":"Test cases are work products that can and should be reviewed statically."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Almost any readable work product (requirements, design, code, models, test cases, user guides, contracts) can be statically tested. What cannot is behavior that only emerges during execution, such as memory leaks, performance, or timing.$L$, $L$A team reviews their Gherkin feature files for clarity, but must actually run the app to see a memory leak surface after an hour of use.$L$
