select c.id, $L$m3-03$L$, $L$Static Testing$L$, 3, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$Why does static testing often provide a better return on investment than finding the same defects later?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Defects found early are cheaper to fix because they have not yet propagated into downstream work products","correct":true,"why":"Early removal avoids the compounding cost of a defect that flows into design, code, and tests before discovery."},{"text":"Static testing always finds more defects than dynamic testing","correct":false,"why":"Neither technique universally finds more; they find different kinds of defects."},{"text":"Static testing eliminates the need for any dynamic testing","correct":false,"why":"The two are complementary; static testing does not replace execution-based testing."},{"text":"Static testing removes the need to write test cases","correct":false,"why":"Test cases are still needed; in fact they are themselves reviewed statically."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$The cost of a defect rises the longer it stays undetected. Static testing can find defects in requirements before code exists, preventing expensive rework downstream.$L$, $L$A reviewer spots a contradictory acceptance criterion in a story, saving the team from building and later re-building a feature the wrong way.$L$
