select c.id, $L$m3-25$L$, $L$Static Testing$L$, 3, $L$K3$L$, null, null, $L$A team must review a large, safety-critical requirements specification that will drive contracts and downstream design, and management wants defect metrics to improve the process. Which review type should they apply?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Inspection, because it provides the most formal, metric-driven process suited to a critical, high-impact document","correct":true,"why":"The high risk, contractual impact, and need for metrics point to the most formal type, an inspection."},{"text":"Informal review, because it is quick and requires no documentation","correct":false,"why":"An informal review lacks the rigor and metrics needed for a safety-critical, contract-driving document."},{"text":"Walkthrough, because the author can simply explain the document","correct":false,"why":"An author-led walkthrough does not provide the formal rules, roles, and metrics management requires."},{"text":"No review, because dynamic testing will catch the issues later","correct":false,"why":"Requirements cannot be dynamically tested and late discovery would be very costly for a critical document."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Selecting a review type means matching formality to risk, objectives, and the need for metrics. A safety-critical, contract-relevant specification with a metrics goal calls for an inspection, the most formal review type.$L$, $L$For a medical-device requirements set feeding supplier contracts, the QA lead schedules a full inspection with a moderator, checklists, entry and exit criteria, and logged defect data.$L$
