select c.id, $L$m6-09$L$, $L$Test Management$L$, 6, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$Which content is most appropriate for a test progress report rather than a test completion report?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Status of testing in the current period, including impediments and deviations so far","correct":true,"why":"Progress reports focus on the current status, blocking issues and any deviations during the ongoing period so control actions can be taken."},{"text":"A final evaluation of whether exit criteria were met for the whole level","correct":false,"why":"A final evaluation against exit criteria is characteristic of the completion report at the end of the effort."},{"text":"The complete lessons learned from the entire project","correct":false,"why":"Full lessons learned belong in the completion report, which reflects on the finished effort."},{"text":"The archived residual risks handed over to operations at release","correct":false,"why":"Handover of residual risks at release is part of the completion report content."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Progress reports cover the current reporting period so stakeholders can steer the effort; completion reports summarize and evaluate the whole test effort at its end. Choosing the right content depends on which report you are writing.$L$, $L$The mid-sprint progress report flags that a blocked test environment is delaying execution, prompting the manager to escalate, whereas the sprint-end completion report evaluates overall results.$L$
