select c.id, $L$m6-17$L$, $L$Test Management$L$, 6, $L$K3$L$, null, null, $L$A banking release has limited time. The login screen is low risk, but the funds-transfer feature is high risk. How should a risk-based approach guide test management?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Allocate more, deeper testing to funds transfer and lighter testing to login, prioritizing by risk","correct":true,"why":"Risk-based testing concentrates effort where failure impact and likelihood are highest, so the high-risk funds transfer feature gets deeper testing."},{"text":"Test both features with identical depth regardless of risk to be fair","correct":false,"why":"Equal effort ignores risk and wastes scarce time on the low-risk feature instead of the critical one."},{"text":"Test only the login screen because it is quicker to test","correct":false,"why":"Choosing by convenience rather than risk leaves the highest-risk feature under-tested."},{"text":"Skip risk analysis and test whatever the developers finish first","correct":false,"why":"Testing by build order rather than risk fails to protect the most critical functionality."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Risk-based test management uses product risk to prioritize and allocate effort. Under time pressure, the highest-risk items receive the most thorough testing while lower-risk items receive proportionally less.$L$, $L$With two days before release, the test manager schedules extensive boundary, security and negative testing on funds transfer and only a short smoke test on the stable login page.$L$
