select c.id, $L$m7-17$L$, $L$Defect Management$L$, 7, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$Which rewrite of a defect summary BEST reflects professional, blame-free communication?$L$, $J$[{"text":"'On build 5.3, submitting the form with an empty email field saves the record instead of showing the required validation error.'","correct":true,"why":"It is factual and specific: build, action, expected versus actual behaviour, with no personal attribution, which is exactly what good defect communication requires."},{"text":"'Someone broke the form validation again, as usual.'","correct":false,"why":"It is vague and accusatory, gives no reproduction detail, and assigns blame rather than describing the anomaly."},{"text":"'The form is a mess and whoever wrote it should be ashamed.'","correct":false,"why":"It is emotive and insulting, contains no actionable technical detail, and violates no-blame principles."},{"text":"'Validation is broken, figure it out yourselves.'","correct":false,"why":"It is dismissive and unhelpful, providing neither reproduction steps nor expected/actual results."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$A good defect summary states facts (environment, action, expected and actual results) neutrally and specifically, enabling the reader to act without ambiguity or defensiveness.$L$, $L$A tester adopts a summary template of 'On [build], when [action], expected [X] but got [Y]', and triage discussions become faster and less tense.$L$
