select c.id, $L$m8-23$L$, $L$Risk-Based Testing$L$, 8, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$How does risk-based testing typically use the results of risk analysis when planning?$L$, $J$[{"text":"To select techniques, allocate effort, and sequence testing according to each area's risk level","correct":true,"why":"Risk analysis informs which techniques to apply, how much effort to spend, and the order of testing, all scaled to risk."},{"text":"To decide the colour scheme of the test report","correct":false,"why":"Presentation styling is unrelated to how risk analysis drives test planning."},{"text":"To set the office seating plan for the test team","correct":false,"why":"Logistics of seating have nothing to do with using risk analysis to plan testing."},{"text":"To fix the number of testers permanently regardless of findings","correct":false,"why":"Risk analysis informs flexible allocation; it does not lock staffing irrespective of the risk picture."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$The output of risk analysis feeds test planning in several ways: choosing more rigorous techniques for high-risk areas, allocating more effort there, and ordering tests so high-risk items run first. This makes testing efficient and focused.$L$, $L$The plan assigns experienced testers and exploratory plus scripted techniques to high-risk modules, while low-risk areas get a brief checklist review.$L$
