  (course_id, ext_key, syllabus_topic, module_number, k_level, mock, mock_order, question_text, options, explanation, workplace_example)
select c.id, $L$m9-10$L$, $L$Test Tools$L$, 9, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$Which statement about the benefits of test automation is OVERSTATED and therefore incorrect?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Automation will find all the important defects that manual testing would miss","correct":true,"why":"This is an unrealistic expectation; automated tests only check what they were written to check and often miss defects a human would notice."},{"text":"Automation can provide more consistent and repeatable test execution","correct":false,"why":"Consistency and repeatability are genuine, well-documented benefits of automation."},{"text":"Automation can give faster feedback on regression issues","correct":false,"why":"Faster regression feedback is a real benefit of automation."},{"text":"Automation can enable objective measurement, such as coverage figures","correct":false,"why":"Objective measurement is a legitimate benefit of tool-supported testing."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$A common trap is expecting automation to find defects manual testing misses. Automated tests only verify the specific conditions they encode; they rarely notice the unexpected. Overstated benefits like this create unrealistic expectations and later disappointment.$L$, $L$A manager promises the board that automation will 'catch everything'. Two releases later a glaring layout bug ships because no script checked visual appearance - a human tester would have spotted it in seconds. The over-promise damaged trust in the whole automation effort.$L$
