select c.id, $L$m9-12$L$, $L$Test Tools$L$, 9, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$A team stops doing any exploratory or manual testing because they believe their automated suite covers everything. Which automation risk does this illustrate?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Over-reliance on automation","correct":true,"why":"Trusting automation to cover everything and abandoning human testing is the classic over-reliance risk."},{"text":"Under-investment in the automation tool licence","correct":false,"why":"The problem here is behavioural over-trust, not a licensing shortfall."},{"text":"Excessive manual regression effort","correct":false,"why":"The team has reduced manual effort; the issue is that they have reduced it too far."},{"text":"Lack of any test data","correct":false,"why":"Test data is not the issue described; the issue is misplaced confidence in automation."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Over-reliance means treating a green automated suite as proof of quality and dropping the human testing that catches the unexpected. Automation checks known conditions; exploratory testing finds the unknowns. Both are needed.$L$, $L$Because the automated suite was always green, a team dropped exploratory testing. A confusing new checkout flow shipped that technically worked but frustrated users - no script questioned the user experience, and no human was looking any more.$L$
