select c.id, $L$m9-13$L$, $L$Test Tools$L$, 9, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$Why is it risky to set an expectation that automation will 'pay for itself immediately' in the first release?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Automation requires significant up-front investment, so returns typically appear over multiple cycles, not instantly","correct":true,"why":"Setup, framework and scripting effort are paid up front; the payback accrues as tests are re-run across many cycles."},{"text":"Automated tests can only ever run once","correct":false,"why":"Automated tests are valuable precisely because they can be re-run many times."},{"text":"Automation makes the software slower to build","correct":false,"why":"Automation does not inherently slow the build; that is not the reason returns are delayed."},{"text":"Automation reduces test coverage on purpose","correct":false,"why":"Automation aims to support coverage, not deliberately reduce it."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Unrealistic expectations - like instant payback - are a named automation risk. The initial investment in tools, frameworks and scripting is high; value comes from repeated re-execution over time. Setting expectations correctly protects the initiative from being judged a failure too early.$L$, $L$A team's first automated release actually costs more than manual testing because of framework setup. By the fifth release the same scripts have run twenty times for near-zero effort, and the cumulative saving is large - the payback was real but not immediate.$L$
