select c.id, $L$m9-14$L$, $L$Test Tools$L$, 9, $L$K3$L$, null, null, $L$A company evaluating whether to adopt a new test execution tool wants to reduce the risk of a bad organisation-wide rollout. Which action BEST addresses this concern?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Run a pilot project on a limited scope to learn the tool and assess fit before wider deployment","correct":true,"why":"A pilot lets the organisation gain experience, evaluate fit and uncover issues on a small scale before committing widely."},{"text":"Deploy the tool to every team on day one to maximise adoption speed","correct":false,"why":"Big-bang deployment amplifies the impact of any problem and skips the learning a pilot provides."},{"text":"Buy the most expensive tool available on the assumption it must be best","correct":false,"why":"Cost is not a reliable proxy for fit; selection should be based on evaluation against needs."},{"text":"Automate all existing manual tests before evaluating the tool at all","correct":false,"why":"Mass automation before any evaluation risks large wasted effort if the tool proves unsuitable."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$ISTQB recommends introducing a tool via a pilot project. A pilot builds hands-on knowledge, evaluates how well the tool fits existing processes and technology, identifies needed process changes, and assesses the true cost and benefit - all before a risky full-scale rollout.$L$, $L$Before committing, a QA manager pilots a new automation tool on one low-risk product for six weeks. The pilot reveals the tool struggles with the company's legacy desktop app - a finding that saves the organisation from a costly mistake across ten teams.$L$
