select c.id, $L$m9-15$L$, $L$Test Tools$L$, 9, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$Which of the following is a key GOAL of running a pilot project when introducing a new test tool?$L$, $J$[{"text":"To gain experience with the tool and learn how it fits the organisation's processes and technology","correct":true,"why":"A pilot's central aims are learning the tool, assessing fit, and identifying process changes and realistic costs and benefits."},{"text":"To immediately replace all testers with the tool","correct":false,"why":"Tools support testers; a pilot does not aim to eliminate the test team."},{"text":"To avoid ever having to change existing processes","correct":false,"why":"A pilot often reveals that processes must adapt to use the tool effectively."},{"text":"To lock in a purchase before any evaluation is done","correct":false,"why":"The pilot is part of the evaluation; it exists to inform, not bypass, the decision."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$A pilot project evaluates the tool against real needs, builds skills, determines how ways of working must adapt, and assesses whether expected benefits are realistic. Its findings drive a well-informed decision about full deployment.$L$, $L$During a pilot, a team discovers the new tool needs test cases written in a specific format. Rather than abandon the tool, they adapt their test-authoring process - a change they would never have identified without the pilot.$L$
