select c.id, $L$m9-16$L$, $L$Test Tools$L$, 9, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$When introducing a test tool across an organisation, why is coaching and mentoring of staff considered important?$L$, $J$[{"text":"It helps testers build the skills and confidence to use the tool effectively, improving adoption and return on investment","correct":true,"why":"Coaching and mentoring support new users, spread good practice and are a recognised success factor for tool introduction."},{"text":"It removes the need to ever adapt existing processes","correct":false,"why":"Successful introduction usually requires process changes; coaching supports those changes rather than avoiding them."},{"text":"It guarantees the tool will find every defect","correct":false,"why":"No amount of coaching makes a tool find every defect; that is an unrealistic expectation."},{"text":"It makes the tool free to license","correct":false,"why":"Coaching addresses skills and adoption, not licensing cost."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Introducing a tool is an organisational change, not just an installation. Providing coaching, mentoring and support helps staff use the tool well, encourages consistent good practice, and is central to realising the tool's expected benefits.$L$, $L$After buying an automation tool, a company pairs each team with an experienced mentor for a month. Adoption jumps because testers get help over the first hurdles, instead of quietly giving up and reverting to manual work.$L$
