select c.id, $L$m9-17$L$, $L$Test Tools$L$, 9, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$Which factor is a legitimate SPECIAL CONSIDERATION when SELECTING a test tool for an organisation?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Assessing how well the tool fits the organisation's existing processes, technology and skills","correct":true,"why":"Fit with current processes, technology and team maturity is a core selection consideration in ISTQB."},{"text":"Choosing whichever tool a competitor happens to use, without evaluating fit","correct":false,"why":"Copying a competitor without evaluating fit ignores the organisation's own context and needs."},{"text":"Selecting a tool purely because it has the largest feature list","correct":false,"why":"A long feature list does not mean the tool fits the organisation's actual needs and constraints."},{"text":"Assuming any tool will integrate perfectly with all existing systems","correct":false,"why":"Integration must be assessed, not assumed; poor integration is a common cause of failed adoption."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Tool selection considers the organisation's maturity, strengths and weaknesses; how the tool fits existing processes and technology; the vendor and support arrangements; integration needs; licensing model; and a clear cost-benefit case - not just feature counts.$L$, $L$A team assesses two tools against a checklist: fit with their CI pipeline, support quality, licence cost and required skills. The flashier tool loses because it cannot integrate with their existing pipeline - fit beat features.$L$
