select c.id, $L$m9-18$L$, $L$Test Tools$L$, 9, $L$K1$L$, null, null, $L$When introducing a new test tool, why should an organisation often adapt or improve its processes rather than expect the tool to slot in unchanged?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Because effective tool use frequently requires supporting processes to change so the tool delivers real value","correct":true,"why":"ISTQB stresses that introducing a tool usually requires adapting and improving processes to fit the new way of working."},{"text":"Because tools legally require documented process rewrites","correct":false,"why":"There is no legal requirement; the reason is practical effectiveness, not regulation."},{"text":"Because processes must always be discarded when a tool arrives","correct":false,"why":"Processes are adapted and improved, not necessarily discarded wholesale."},{"text":"Because the tool vendor forbids using existing processes","correct":false,"why":"Vendors do not forbid existing processes; adaptation is driven by fit and effectiveness."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$A tool imposed on unsuitable processes rarely delivers. Successful introduction adapts and improves ways of working so the tool fits - for example changing how test cases are written or when tests are triggered - to unlock the intended benefits.$L$, $L$A team adopts a tool that runs tests automatically on each commit. To benefit, they change their process so tests are committed alongside code - a small workflow change that turns the tool from shelfware into a fast feedback engine.$L$
