select c.id, $L$m9-22$L$, $L$Test Tools$L$, 9, $L$K3$L$, null, null, $L$A team has a stable, rarely changing set of core business rules that must be re-verified on every release, plus a brand-new experimental feature whose UI changes daily. How should they apply automation MOST sensibly?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Automate the stable core rules for reliable regression, and test the volatile new feature mainly manually for now","correct":true,"why":"Automation pays off on stable, repeatedly executed tests; automating a daily-changing UI would incur crippling maintenance cost."},{"text":"Automate the volatile new feature first because it is the newest","correct":false,"why":"A daily-changing UI would break scripts constantly, generating high maintenance cost for little return."},{"text":"Automate nothing, since some parts of the system change","correct":false,"why":"The stable core rules are ideal automation candidates; avoiding all automation wastes a clear opportunity."},{"text":"Automate everything immediately regardless of how often it changes","correct":false,"why":"Automating volatile areas ignores the maintenance-cost risk and is likely to backfire."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Automation gives the best return on stable tests that are re-executed many times, because maintenance stays low and re-runs are cheap. Volatile, fast-changing areas are poor automation candidates - the maintenance cost of constantly fixing scripts outweighs the benefit, so manual testing is more sensible until they stabilise.$L$, $L$A team automates its unchanging tax-calculation rules, which run green on every release for months. They test the daily-shifting new dashboard by hand, waiting until its design settles before writing scripts - saving themselves from rewriting broken tests every single day.$L$
