select c.id, $L$m9-23$L$, $L$Test Tools$L$, 9, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$An organisation reports that its automation project failed because 'the tool did not deliver the massive savings promised in the sales demo'. Which underlying cause does this MOST likely reflect?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Unrealistic expectations were set about what the tool would achieve","correct":true,"why":"Judging a tool against exaggerated, unrealistic promises is a named automation risk and a common cause of perceived failure."},{"text":"The tool was too cheap to be useful","correct":false,"why":"Price is not the issue; the failure stems from mismatched expectations versus reality."},{"text":"Automation inherently cannot save any effort","correct":false,"why":"Automation genuinely can save effort over time; the problem is the scale and speed that were promised."},{"text":"The organisation used the tool for regression testing","correct":false,"why":"Regression is a sound use of automation; it is not the cause of the perceived failure."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Setting unrealistic expectations - often fuelled by polished vendor demos - is a classic reason automation is judged a failure. The tool may deliver real, gradual value, but if it is measured against impossible promises it will always seem to fall short. Honest expectation-setting is a key success factor.$L$, $L$A vendor demo promised '90% effort reduction from day one'. A year in, automation had cut regression effort by a solid 40% over time - a genuine win - yet management called it a failure because it never matched the demo's inflated claim.$L$
