select c.id, $L$m9-24$L$, $L$Test Tools$L$, 9, $L$K1$L$, null, null, $L$Which of the following is a recognised risk of depending heavily on a single test tool from one vendor?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Dependence on the tool vendor, who may raise prices, drop support, or discontinue the product","correct":true,"why":"Vendor dependence - including price rises, withdrawn support or a discontinued product - is a named risk of tool adoption."},{"text":"The tool becomes impossible to license under any circumstances","correct":false,"why":"Licensing remains possible; the risk is reliance on the vendor's continued goodwill and support."},{"text":"The organisation is legally barred from testing manually","correct":false,"why":"There is no such legal bar; manual testing remains available."},{"text":"The tool will automatically improve without any maintenance","correct":false,"why":"Tools and their tests still need maintenance; this is not a benefit that offsets vendor risk."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Relying on a proprietary tool ties the organisation to its vendor. If the vendor increases prices, reduces support, changes direction or discontinues the product, the organisation is exposed. This is why vendor stability and support are part of tool selection.$L$, $L$A team built its entire regression suite in a proprietary tool. When the vendor doubled the licence fee and dropped support for the team's platform, they faced an expensive migration - a painful lesson in vendor dependence.$L$
