select c.id, $L$m10-02$L$, $L$Agile Testing$L$, 10, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$Why does the agile emphasis on responding to change (over following a fixed plan) affect testers?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Requirements and priorities can evolve each iteration, so test work must adapt continuously","correct":true,"why":"Because scope can change between iterations, testers must re-assess risks and update or add tests frequently rather than working to a frozen test plan."},{"text":"It means testers no longer need any form of test planning","correct":false,"why":"Planning still happens; it becomes lighter and more frequent, not absent."},{"text":"It removes the need to prioritise tests because everything is tested equally","correct":false,"why":"Change increases the need to prioritise by risk, not remove it."},{"text":"It guarantees requirements will never change once an iteration starts","correct":false,"why":"Agile explicitly embraces change; it does not guarantee stability."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Agile values responding to change over rigidly following a plan. For testers this means test conditions, priorities and coverage must be revisited each iteration as the product and backlog evolve, so testing stays aligned with current risk.$L$, $L$When the product owner reprioritises the backlog mid-project, the tester re-evaluates which features carry the most risk and adjusts the test focus for the next iteration accordingly.$L$
