select c.id, $L$m10-03$L$, $L$Agile Testing$L$, 10, $L$K1$L$, null, null, $L$What does the whole-team approach in agile mean for quality?$L$, $J$[{"text":"The entire team shares responsibility for product quality","correct":true,"why":"In the whole-team approach, developers, testers and business roles are collectively accountable for quality, not just a separate test group."},{"text":"Only the testers are accountable for quality","correct":false,"why":"Making testers solely responsible contradicts the whole-team idea."},{"text":"Only the product owner decides whether quality is good enough","correct":false,"why":"The product owner sets priorities but does not hold sole responsibility for quality."},{"text":"Quality is the responsibility of a separate quality assurance department outside the team","correct":false,"why":"The whole-team approach keeps quality responsibility inside the cross-functional team."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$The whole-team approach means anyone with the necessary knowledge and skill contributes to quality, and the whole cross-functional team owns quality collectively. This is a defining feature of testing in agile.$L$, $L$When a defect escapes to production, the team reviews it together in a retrospective and improves their process, rather than blaming the testers alone.$L$
