select c.id, $L$m10-05$L$, $L$Agile Testing$L$, 10, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$What is a potential risk of the whole-team approach that a team should watch for?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Testing knowledge can be diluted or overlooked if no one champions a rigorous test perspective","correct":true,"why":"When everyone is responsible, specialist test thinking can be lost unless it is deliberately maintained within the team."},{"text":"It always makes the team too large to communicate","correct":false,"why":"Whole-team working typically favours small teams, so size is not the inherent risk."},{"text":"It forces the team to abandon automation","correct":false,"why":"The whole-team approach is compatible with, and often encourages, automation."},{"text":"It prevents developers from ever writing tests","correct":false,"why":"The approach encourages developers to contribute to testing, not the reverse."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$A recognised risk of shared responsibility is that when "everyone" owns quality, specialist testing skills and a critical test mindset can be under-applied. Teams mitigate this by ensuring test expertise is present and valued.$L$, $L$A team where developers write only happy-path checks realises negative and edge-case testing is being skipped, and asks the tester to coach the team on stronger test design.$L$
