select c.id, $L$m10-08$L$, $L$Agile Testing$L$, 10, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$Why is exploratory testing especially valuable within short agile iterations?$L$, $J$[{"text":"It provides fast feedback on new features when there is little time for extensive scripted test preparation","correct":true,"why":"Exploratory testing combines learning, design and execution simultaneously, giving quick coverage of newly built features."},{"text":"It removes the need to think about test conditions at all","correct":false,"why":"Exploratory testing is structured thinking on the fly, not the absence of thought."},{"text":"It replaces the need for any automated regression testing","correct":false,"why":"Exploratory testing complements automation; it does not replace regression automation."},{"text":"It guarantees complete coverage of every possible input","correct":false,"why":"No technique guarantees complete coverage; exhaustive testing is impractical."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Exploratory testing suits agile because tester learning, test design and execution happen together, giving rapid feedback on freshly delivered functionality when there is limited time to prepare detailed scripts. It works alongside, not instead of, automation.$L$, $L$As soon as a developer finishes a checkout feature, the tester runs a timeboxed exploratory session using a charter, quickly surfacing an issue with an unusual discount code.$L$
