select c.id, $L$m10-10$L$, $L$Agile Testing$L$, 10, $L$K1$L$, null, null, $L$In Acceptance Test-Driven Development (ATDD), when are acceptance tests typically defined?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Collaboratively, before development of the feature begins","correct":true,"why":"ATDD defines acceptance tests up front through collaboration, so the tests express the agreed expected behaviour before coding."},{"text":"Only after the feature has been released to production","correct":false,"why":"Defining acceptance tests only post-release defeats the purpose of driving development with them."},{"text":"By the testers alone with no input from other roles","correct":false,"why":"ATDD is collaborative, involving business, development and test perspectives together."},{"text":"Never, because ATDD does not use acceptance tests","correct":false,"why":"Acceptance tests are the central artefact of ATDD."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$ATDD is a collaborative, test-first approach in which acceptance tests are created together (business, developer and tester) before development starts. The tests capture the agreed criteria and guide the implementation.$L$, $L$Before coding a login feature, the product owner, developer and tester agree concrete acceptance tests, such as the behaviour after three failed attempts, and those tests drive the work.$L$
