select c.id, $L$m10-11$L$, $L$Agile Testing$L$, 10, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$How does Behaviour-Driven Development (BDD) support communication about expected behaviour?$L$, $J$[{"text":"It expresses expected behaviour in structured, readable scenarios that business and technical people can share","correct":true,"why":"BDD scenarios use a common, structured natural-language style so all roles understand and agree on the intended behaviour."},{"text":"It hides the expected behaviour in code only developers can read","correct":false,"why":"BDD deliberately makes behaviour readable to non-technical stakeholders."},{"text":"It removes the need to agree on requirements","correct":false,"why":"BDD is a way of agreeing and expressing requirements, not avoiding them."},{"text":"It applies only to performance testing","correct":false,"why":"BDD describes functional behaviour and is not limited to performance testing."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$BDD encourages collaboration by describing desired behaviour in a structured, human-readable scenario format. This shared language helps business and technical team members build a common understanding of what the system should do.$L$, $L$The team writes scenarios in a Given/When/Then style so the product owner can read and confirm the intended behaviour of a refund rule before it is built.$L$
