select c.id, $L$m10-12$L$, $L$Agile Testing$L$, 10, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$Why is a risk-based approach useful when prioritising testing in an agile iteration?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Limited time means testing effort should focus first on the areas of highest risk","correct":true,"why":"Short iterations rarely allow testing everything, so aligning effort with risk maximises the value of the time available."},{"text":"It lets the team test every path exhaustively within the sprint","correct":false,"why":"Exhaustive testing is not feasible; risk-based prioritisation exists precisely because time is limited."},{"text":"It means low-risk items must always be tested first","correct":false,"why":"Risk-based prioritisation focuses on higher-risk items first, not lower-risk ones."},{"text":"It removes the need to test high-risk features","correct":false,"why":"Risk-based testing directs more effort to high-risk features, not less."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Because agile iterations are short and time is constrained, testers use risk to decide what to test and how thoroughly. Focusing on the highest-risk areas first ensures the most important defects are more likely to be found early.$L$, $L$With only a few days of testing left in the sprint, the tester concentrates on the payment flow (high business risk) before spending time on a cosmetic settings screen.$L$
