select c.id, $L$m10-13$L$, $L$Agile Testing$L$, 10, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$From a testing viewpoint, what is a key value of iteration planning in agile?$L$, $J$[{"text":"The team agrees the scope of the iteration so testers can plan test effort and identify risks for those items","correct":true,"why":"Knowing which stories are in the iteration lets testers estimate test effort, spot risks and prepare test conditions for that scope."},{"text":"It fixes the entire project scope so no change is ever possible","correct":false,"why":"Iteration planning covers one iteration and does not freeze the whole project."},{"text":"It is where all detailed test scripts for the whole release are finalised","correct":false,"why":"Test design in agile is done just-in-time, not all finalised at one planning event."},{"text":"It excludes testers because it is only for developers","correct":false,"why":"Testers participate in iteration planning to raise testability and risk concerns."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Iteration (sprint) planning defines the goal and the set of backlog items for the coming iteration. For testers this is where they help assess testability, estimate testing effort, and identify risks in the selected stories.$L$, $L$During sprint planning the tester flags that one story lacks clear acceptance criteria, so the team refines it before committing, avoiding wasted effort later.$L$
