select c.id, $L$m10-17$L$, $L$Agile Testing$L$, 10, $L$K1$L$, null, null, $L$What is continuous integration (CI)?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Frequently merging code changes and automatically building and testing them","correct":true,"why":"CI integrates changes often and runs an automated build with tests each time, giving rapid feedback on integration problems."},{"text":"Deploying to production manually once per year","correct":false,"why":"That is neither continuous nor integration-focused."},{"text":"Writing all code first and integrating it only at the very end","correct":false,"why":"Late one-off integration is the opposite of continuous integration."},{"text":"A manual process with no automated tests","correct":false,"why":"Automated build and test are what make CI valuable."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Continuous integration means developers integrate their changes frequently, triggering an automated build and a suite of automated tests each time. This provides fast feedback so integration defects are caught early.$L$, $L$Every time a developer pushes code, the CI server compiles the build and runs unit and integration tests, alerting the team within minutes if something breaks.$L$
