select c.id, $L$m10-18$L$, $L$Agile Testing$L$, 10, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$How does continuous integration benefit testing in an agile context?$L$, $J$[{"text":"It gives rapid feedback on the quality of each change, so integration defects are found early","correct":true,"why":"Automated build and test on each integration surface problems quickly, reducing the cost of fixing them."},{"text":"It removes the need for any regression testing","correct":false,"why":"CI actually relies on and runs regression tests; it does not remove the need for them."},{"text":"It guarantees that the build will never fail","correct":false,"why":"CI often surfaces failures; that early detection is the benefit, not a promise of no failures."},{"text":"It means testers no longer need to design tests","correct":false,"why":"Someone must design the automated tests CI runs; test design remains essential."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$CI benefits testing by executing automated tests on every integration, providing fast feedback and enabling early detection of integration and regression defects. It reduces integration risk but depends on well-designed automated tests.$L$, $L$A change that breaks an existing feature is caught by the CI regression suite within minutes of being pushed, so the developer fixes it before it reaches the tester.$L$
