select c.id, $L$m10-24$L$, $L$Agile Testing$L$, 10, $L$K3$L$, null, null, $L$A two-week iteration is nearly over. A high-risk payment defect was just fixed, several stories changed shared code, and there is only one day of testing left. What is the most appropriate testing response?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Confirmation-test the payment fix, then run automated regression focused on the highest-risk changed areas","correct":true,"why":"This confirms the specific fix and uses risk-based, automated regression to cover the most important side effects within the limited time."},{"text":"Skip confirmation testing and manually re-test every feature in the product from scratch","correct":false,"why":"Manually re-testing everything is infeasible in one day and skipping the fix check leaves the key risk unverified."},{"text":"Do nothing extra because continuous integration already guarantees correctness","correct":false,"why":"CI provides fast feedback but does not guarantee correctness, and the high-risk fix still needs confirming."},{"text":"Run only the cosmetic UI checks and defer all payment testing to next iteration","correct":false,"why":"Deferring the high-risk payment area contradicts risk-based prioritisation."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$With limited time, the tester should first confirm the high-risk fix works (confirmation testing), then apply risk-based regression, using automation, to the areas most likely affected by the shared-code changes. This combines confirmation testing, regression testing, risk-based prioritisation and automation appropriately.$L$, $L$On the last day of the sprint the tester re-runs the failing payment scenario to confirm the fix, then triggers the automated regression suite against the payment and checkout modules that shared the changed code.$L$
