select c.id, $L$m10-25$L$, $L$Agile Testing$L$, 10, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$How can a tester best maintain independence of perspective while still being a fully collaborative member of an agile team?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Collaborate closely on stories while still critically questioning assumptions and designing independent tests","correct":true,"why":"Independence in agile is about perspective and questioning, which can coexist with close day-to-day collaboration."},{"text":"Refuse to talk to developers so as not to be influenced by them","correct":false,"why":"Avoiding communication harms collaboration and is not how agile independence works."},{"text":"Only test after leaving the team so there is no contact","correct":false,"why":"Agile testers are embedded in the team; leaving it is not the goal."},{"text":"Accept the developers' assumptions without challenge to keep harmony","correct":false,"why":"Accepting assumptions unquestioned surrenders the independent perspective entirely."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Independence of testing in agile is preserved through mindset, not isolation. A tester stays collaborative yet keeps a questioning, critical perspective and designs tests that challenge assumptions, gaining the benefits of independence without sacrificing teamwork.$L$, $L$The tester pairs happily with developers all sprint, yet still designs negative and boundary tests that deliberately challenge the assumptions built into the code.$L$
