-- Module 11: Real World QA (25)
insert into public.practice_questions
  (course_id, ext_key, syllabus_topic, module_number, k_level, mock, mock_order, question_text, options, explanation, workplace_example)
select c.id, $L$m11-01$L$, $L$Real World QA$L$, 11, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$Two days before release, a developer says: "The login module has passed every test for three sprints, so let's stop testing it and reassign the tester." As the QA lead, which ISTQB principle most directly warns against relying on this reasoning to declare the module defect-free?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Testing shows the presence of defects, not their absence","correct":true,"why":"Passing tests can only demonstrate that defects were not found by those tests; they can never prove the module has no remaining defects, so 'defect-free' is an unsupportable claim."},{"text":"Exhaustive testing is impossible","correct":false,"why":"True as a principle, but it addresses why we cannot test everything, not why passing tests fail to prove absence of defects in what was tested."},{"text":"Tests wear out","correct":false,"why":"This is not an ISTQB principle. The pesticide paradox describes repeated tests finding fewer new defects, which is a different point from proving absence of defects."},{"text":"Testing is context dependent","correct":false,"why":"This principle says testing differs by context; it does not address the logical limit of what passing tests can prove."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$The principle 'testing shows the presence of defects, not their absence' directly rebuts any claim that a clean test run proves a component is bug-free. You can reduce the probability of undiscovered defects, but never guarantee their absence. The right response is to base any stop-testing decision on risk and exit criteria, not on the false conclusion that no failures means no defects.$L$, $L$The same trap appears when a manager says 'the smoke test is green, so we're good to ship.' A green smoke test covers a thin slice of behaviour; it is evidence of the absence of the failures you looked for, not evidence of overall correctness. On the job, phrase status as 'no known blocking defects in the areas we tested,' never as 'no defects.'$L$
