insert into public.practice_questions
  (course_id, ext_key, syllabus_topic, module_number, k_level, mock, mock_order, question_text, options, explanation, workplace_example)
select c.id, $L$m11-02$L$, $L$Real World QA$L$, 11, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$Your regression pack takes eleven hours to run and has grown for two years, yet it now catches almost no new defects while new bugs keep slipping through in recently changed areas. Which ISTQB principle best explains this, and what is the aligned response?$L$, $J$[{"text":"The pesticide paradox applies; review and revise the tests, adding new cases for changed and risky areas","correct":true,"why":"Repeating the same tests stops finding new defects over time; the syllabus response is to regularly review and update the test cases to exercise different paths and newly changed code."},{"text":"Defect clustering applies; delete the regression pack because defects are elsewhere","correct":false,"why":"Clustering is real, but deleting an entire regression pack discards protection; the paradox here calls for revising tests, not removing coverage."},{"text":"Early testing applies; move all tests to the start of the project","correct":false,"why":"Early testing is valuable but does not explain why an existing repeated suite stops finding defects."},{"text":"Exhaustive testing is impossible; therefore stop maintaining the suite","correct":false,"why":"That principle justifies prioritisation, not abandoning maintenance of a suite that is going stale."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$The pesticide paradox states that running the same tests repeatedly eventually stops revealing new defects. The syllabus remedy is to review and revise tests regularly and write new ones to exercise different parts of the software, especially changed and higher-risk areas. That, combined with defect-clustering insight to aim effort where bugs concentrate, keeps the suite effective instead of merely long.$L$, $L$A team I worked with had a four-hour suite that was 90 percent duplicate assertions and never touched the payment refactor shipped last quarter. Pruning stale cases and adding targeted tests for the changed code cut runtime and immediately caught two refund defects. Longer suites are not safer suites; relevance beats volume.$L$
