insert into public.practice_questions
  (course_id, ext_key, syllabus_topic, module_number, k_level, mock, mock_order, question_text, options, explanation, workplace_example)
select c.id, $L$m11-03$L$, $L$Real World QA$L$, 11, $L$K3$L$, null, null, $L$In a defect triage meeting, a cosmetic typo on the internal admin dashboard is logged, and separately a checkout total miscalculates VAT for one rare currency used by a single high-value client shipping next week. How should you classify these using severity and priority?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Typo: low severity, low priority. VAT bug: high severity, high priority","correct":true,"why":"Severity reflects impact on the system (wrong money is high impact; a typo is low), and priority reflects business urgency (the VAT bug blocks an imminent high-value shipment, the typo does not)."},{"text":"Both are high priority because all defects must be fixed before release","correct":false,"why":"The zero-defect fallacy: not every defect must be fixed, and treating all as equally urgent ignores risk-based prioritisation."},{"text":"Typo: high priority because it is visible; VAT bug: low priority because it is rare","correct":false,"why":"Visibility on an internal screen is not urgency, and rarity does not lower the impact of miscalculating money for an imminent client."},{"text":"Severity and priority are the same, so rank both as medium","correct":false,"why":"Severity (technical impact) and priority (business urgency) are distinct attributes and can differ for the same defect."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Severity measures the impact of the defect on the system or its users; priority measures how urgently it should be addressed from a business standpoint. They are independent: a wrong-money defect is high severity, and because it affects an imminent high-value shipment it is also high priority. The internal typo is low on both. Risk-based thinking, not visibility or gut feel, drives the ordering.$L$, $L$A classic split is a crash in a feature nobody uses (high severity, low priority) versus a misspelled company name on the landing page before a press launch (low severity, high priority). On the job, always record the two attributes separately so triage can argue urgency without re-arguing impact.$L$
