select c.id, $L$m11-04$L$, $L$Real World QA$L$, 11, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$A product owner asks you to sign off that the release has 'no bugs.' The team has run risk-based testing, met the agreed exit criteria, and has three known low-severity defects deferred with sign-off. What is the most ISTQB-aligned way to report status?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Report that exit criteria are met and there are no known blocking defects, listing the three deferred low-severity defects and residual risk","correct":true,"why":"This is honest, evidence-based, and reflects that testing cannot prove the absence of defects; it communicates coverage, known issues, and residual risk for an informed release decision."},{"text":"Confirm there are no bugs because all planned tests passed","correct":false,"why":"Claiming 'no bugs' violates the principle that testing shows the presence, not the absence, of defects, and hides the known deferred defects."},{"text":"Refuse to report anything until every possible test has been executed","correct":false,"why":"Exhaustive testing is impossible, so waiting for it is never a valid basis for a status report."},{"text":"Say the release is fine because the customer has not complained yet","correct":false,"why":"Absence of complaints is not test evidence and says nothing about coverage or residual risk."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Test status should communicate what was covered, which exit criteria were met, what defects are known and deferred, and what residual risk remains. That gives stakeholders the information to make a release decision. Signing off 'no bugs' is both technically false, given the principle on absence of defects, and dishonest given the known deferred issues.$L$, $L$In a release readiness review, a strong test summary reads: 'Priority-1 and Priority-2 risks covered, exit criteria met, three Priority-4 cosmetic defects deferred with PO approval, residual risk low in reporting exports.' That lets the business own the go decision. 'No bugs' invites blame the first time production hiccups.$L$
