select c.id, $L$m11-06$L$, $L$Real World QA$L$, 11, $L$K3$L$, null, null, $L$A feature lets a support ticket move through states: New -> Open -> Pending -> Resolved -> Closed, with some transitions forbidden (for example you cannot go from New straight to Closed). Which black-box technique is the best fit to design tests for this behaviour?$L$, $J$[{"text":"State transition testing","correct":true,"why":"The feature is defined by states and the allowed and forbidden transitions between them, which is exactly what state transition testing is designed to cover."},{"text":"Boundary value analysis","correct":false,"why":"BVA targets edges of ordered input ranges, not transitions between named states."},{"text":"Decision table testing","correct":false,"why":"Decision tables model combinations of conditions producing actions; they do not naturally capture sequences of states over time."},{"text":"Use case testing only","correct":false,"why":"Use case testing captures end-to-end business flows but is weaker at systematically covering each valid and invalid state transition."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$State transition testing models a system as states, events, transitions, and resulting states. It is ideal when behaviour depends on history and on which moves are permitted. It also prompts you to test invalid transitions (New -> Closed) that developers often forget to block, which is where many workflow defects hide.$L$, $L$Order lifecycles, approval workflows, and media players (playing/paused/stopped) are all natural state machines. The high-value cases are usually the forbidden transitions: can a refunded order be shipped? Testers who draw the state diagram routinely find gaps the requirements never mentioned.$L$
