select c.id, $L$m11-07$L$, $L$Real World QA$L$, 11, $L$K3$L$, null, null, $L$A loan-approval screen grants or denies credit based on combinations of three conditions: applicant employed (yes/no), credit score above threshold (yes/no), and existing customer (yes/no). Which technique best ensures every relevant combination of conditions is covered?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Decision table testing","correct":true,"why":"Decision tables systematically enumerate combinations of conditions and their resulting actions, which is exactly what a rules-driven approval decision requires."},{"text":"State transition testing","correct":false,"why":"There are no evolving states here, just a set of conditions evaluated together to produce a decision."},{"text":"Boundary value analysis","correct":false,"why":"BVA would test the score threshold edge but ignores the combinatorial logic across the three yes/no conditions."},{"text":"Error guessing","correct":false,"why":"Error guessing is experience-based and unsystematic; it cannot guarantee coverage of all rule combinations."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$When output depends on combinations of conditions, a decision table captures each combination as a column with the expected action, exposing missing or contradictory business rules. It gives systematic coverage of the logic that ad hoc testing would leave patchy. You can still add boundary tests for the score threshold within the same effort.$L$, $L$Insurance eligibility, discount rules, and access-control matrices are prime decision-table territory. Building the table frequently surfaces a combination the business never specified ('employed, low score, existing customer') and forces a real decision before it becomes a production dispute.$L$
