select c.id, $L$m11-08$L$, $L$Real World QA$L$, 11, $L$K3$L$, null, null, $L$Under heavy deadline pressure, you can only test part of a new banking app. The team has a risk analysis showing the funds-transfer flow is high likelihood and high impact, while the help-page footer is low on both. How should risk-based testing shape your plan?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Allocate most effort and depth to the funds-transfer flow and only light checks to the footer","correct":true,"why":"Risk-based testing prioritises test effort by the level of risk, concentrating depth where the likelihood and impact of failure are greatest."},{"text":"Split effort equally across every feature so nothing is neglected","correct":false,"why":"Equal effort ignores the risk analysis and wastes scarce time on low-risk areas at the expense of critical ones."},{"text":"Test the easiest features first to maximise the pass count","correct":false,"why":"Optimising for a high pass rate is vanity; it does not reduce the risk that matters to the business."},{"text":"Test the footer thoroughly first because it is quick to finish","correct":false,"why":"Finishing low-risk work first leaves the highest risk untested if time runs out."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Risk is the combination of the likelihood of a failure and its impact. Risk-based testing uses that assessment to prioritise, sequence, and set the depth of testing, so the most important risks get the most attention and are addressed earliest. Under time pressure this ensures the residual risk at release is as low as possible for the effort available.$L$, $L$Before a fintech launch we ranked flows by money-at-risk and legal exposure: transfers and statements got deep testing, the marketing footer got a glance. When time was cut further, we still shipped confident about the flows that could lose real money. Prioritise by risk, and a shortened window costs you the least-important coverage first.$L$
