select c.id, $L$m11-09$L$, $L$Real World QA$L$, 11, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$Mid-sprint, the acceptance criteria for a story you are testing are ambiguous, and two developers interpret them differently. Applying the ISTQB principle of early testing, what is the highest-value action?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Raise the ambiguity now as a static-testing finding so the requirement is clarified before more code is built on it","correct":true,"why":"Early testing means test and review activities begin as early as possible; finding a requirement defect via review is far cheaper than finding it after coding and integration."},{"text":"Wait until the code is complete, then log any mismatch as a defect","correct":false,"why":"Delaying makes the fix more expensive and lets the ambiguity propagate into code and tests."},{"text":"Pick one interpretation yourself and test only that","correct":false,"why":"Guessing hides the ambiguity and risks validating the wrong behaviour."},{"text":"Skip the story since it is unclear","correct":false,"why":"Skipping abandons a high-risk item rather than resolving the root cause cheaply."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$The early testing principle (shift left) holds that testing activities, including static reviews of requirements, should start as early as possible because the cost of fixing a defect rises the later it is found. An ambiguous requirement is a defect in a work product; catching it in review costs a conversation, whereas catching it after release costs a redesign.$L$, $L$A requirement reading 'the report shows recent orders' hid a disagreement: recent meant 30 days to one dev and this-quarter to another. Raising it in refinement cost five minutes; discovering it after the report shipped would have cost a hotfix and an angry client. Ambiguity is a defect, and review is the cheapest place to kill it.$L$
