select c.id, $L$m11-10$L$, $L$Real World QA$L$, 11, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$Your team debates whether to run a formal requirements review or just let testers find issues later during test execution. Which statement best captures the ISTQB rationale for the static review?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Static testing finds defects in work products directly and earlier, without executing code, reducing later rework","correct":true,"why":"Reviews and other static techniques examine requirements, designs, and code without execution, catching defects at their source before they become costly runtime failures."},{"text":"Static testing replaces the need for any dynamic testing","correct":false,"why":"Static and dynamic testing are complementary; reviews cannot verify runtime behaviour, performance, or integration."},{"text":"Reviews are only useful for finding coding standard violations","correct":false,"why":"Reviews find ambiguities, omissions, inconsistencies, and requirement defects, not just style issues."},{"text":"Dynamic testing always finds defects earlier than reviews","correct":false,"why":"Dynamic testing requires executable code, so it necessarily happens later than a review of the requirement."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Static testing examines work products such as requirements, user stories, and code without executing them, using reviews and static analysis. It can find defects, especially in requirements and design, earlier and more cheaply than dynamic testing, and it finds certain defects (ambiguities, omissions) that dynamic testing cannot easily reveal. It complements rather than replaces execution-based testing.$L$, $L$A one-hour spec review before a payments project caught a missing rule for partial refunds that no test case would have thought to exercise, because the behaviour simply was not built. Reviews find the defects of omission that dynamic testing, which can only test what exists, will never surface.$L$
