select c.id, $L$m11-11$L$, $L$Real World QA$L$, 11, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$A stakeholder pushes to skip a planned test level entirely to hit a date, saying 'the unit tests are green so integration testing is redundant.' Which ISTQB-aligned point best challenges this?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Different test levels target different defect types; integration testing finds interface and interaction defects that unit tests by design do not","correct":true,"why":"Each test level (component, integration, system, acceptance) has distinct objectives; passing unit tests says nothing about how components behave together."},{"text":"Integration testing is always more important than unit testing","correct":false,"why":"The syllabus does not rank levels as universally more important; each targets different risks."},{"text":"Green unit tests prove the whole system is defect-free","correct":false,"why":"This repeats the false 'absence of defects' claim and ignores what unit tests scope out."},{"text":"Test levels are interchangeable, so any one is enough","correct":false,"why":"Levels are not interchangeable; they have different objectives and reveal different defects."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Component (unit) testing checks parts in isolation, often with mocks. Integration testing specifically targets defects in the interfaces and interactions between components and systems, which unit tests deliberately stub out. Skipping a level does not remove its risk; it just moves the discovery of those defects to production. The decision should be framed as accepting integration risk, not as removing redundancy.$L$, $L$Two teams each shipped perfectly unit-tested services, but their date formats disagreed at the boundary, and every order timestamp was off by a day. No unit test could catch it because the mock spoke the 'correct' format. Integration testing exists precisely for the seams between green components.$L$
