select c.id, $L$m11-12$L$, $L$Real World QA$L$, 11, $L$K3$L$, null, null, $L$You inherit a flaky end-to-end regression suite: about 8 percent of runs fail intermittently for reasons unrelated to real defects, so the team now ignores red builds. What is the most ISTQB-aligned first step?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Investigate and stabilise or quarantine the flaky tests so a red result reliably signals a real defect","correct":true,"why":"Automated tests are only valuable if their results are trustworthy; unreliable tests undermine the whole feedback loop, so restoring signal integrity comes first."},{"text":"Delete all failing tests to make the build green","correct":false,"why":"Deleting tests removes coverage and hides risk rather than restoring a trustworthy signal."},{"text":"Tell the team to keep ignoring red builds since most failures are false","correct":false,"why":"Habitually ignoring red results means a genuine defect will also be ignored, defeating the purpose of the suite."},{"text":"Add more end-to-end tests to outnumber the flaky ones","correct":false,"why":"Adding volume to an unreliable suite worsens runtime and does nothing to fix trust in the results."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$A key value of test automation is a fast, reliable feedback signal. When results are non-deterministic, people stop trusting them and real regressions slip through. The disciplined response is to diagnose the flakiness (timing, test data, environment), fix or quarantine the offenders, and restore a state where red means a real problem. Only a trustworthy suite supports automated regression decisions.$L$, $L$A suite that cried wolf every third run trained a team to click 'rerun' on red, and they cheerfully reran past a genuine login regression into production. We quarantined 12 timing-dependent tests and fixed the waits; within a sprint a red build meant something again. An ignored test is worse than no test, because it costs runtime and gives false comfort.$L$
