select c.id, $L$m11-13$L$, $L$Real World QA$L$, 11, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$Management wants to automate 100 percent of your manual test cases, including exploratory sessions and one-off usability checks, expecting to eliminate manual testing. Which response best reflects the ISTQB view on automation trade-offs?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Automate stable, repetitive, high-value regression checks, but keep human testers for exploratory, usability, and rapidly changing areas","correct":true,"why":"Automation excels at repeatable, stable checks and frees testers for work requiring judgement and exploration; it complements rather than replaces human testing."},{"text":"Automate everything, since automated tests are always cheaper and better","correct":false,"why":"Automation has build and maintenance costs and cannot replicate exploratory or usability judgement, so 'always cheaper and better' is false."},{"text":"Refuse to automate anything because tests need human judgement","correct":false,"why":"This ignores the real benefits of automating stable, repetitive regression checks."},{"text":"Automate only the newest, most volatile features first","correct":false,"why":"Volatile features change constantly, making automation expensive to maintain; stable regression is the better first target."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Test automation offers benefits (repeatability, speed, coverage of regression, freeing testers for higher-value work) and costs (initial development, ongoing maintenance, tool and skill needs). It suits stable, repetitive, objectively verifiable checks. Exploratory testing and usability assessment rely on human judgement and are poor automation candidates. The goal is the right mix, not total automation.$L$, $L$A team automated a screen that changed every sprint and spent more time fixing tests than testing; meanwhile nobody explored the new checkout, which shipped with a baffling error message a script would happily have marked 'pass.' Automate what is stable and repetitive; let humans explore what is new and subjective.$L$
