select c.id, $L$m11-14$L$, $L$Real World QA$L$, 11, $L$K3$L$, null, null, $L$During a release-readiness meeting, one test suite is at 92 percent pass rate but the 8 percent of failures are all in the payment gateway. Another suite is at 78 percent pass but all failures are trivial cosmetic issues. Which suite's failures should most influence a hold-the-release decision, and why?$L$, $J$[{"text":"The 92 percent suite, because the failing 8 percent are high-risk payment defects regardless of the headline pass rate","correct":true,"why":"Release decisions should weigh the risk and severity of the failing cases, not the raw pass percentage; failing payments are high impact."},{"text":"The 78 percent suite, because a lower pass rate is always worse","correct":false,"why":"Raw pass rate ignores severity; many trivial cosmetic failures may carry far less risk than a few payment failures."},{"text":"Neither, because any suite above 75 percent passes the release gate","correct":false,"why":"A fixed pass-rate threshold with no regard for what failed is not risk-based and can ship critical defects."},{"text":"Whichever suite ran more test cases, since volume signals thoroughness","correct":false,"why":"Case count is not a measure of residual risk or of which failures matter."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Exit criteria and go/no-go decisions must be risk-weighted, not driven by a headline pass percentage. A high pass rate hiding critical failures is more dangerous than a lower pass rate whose failures are trivial. Evaluate what failed, its severity and business impact, and the residual risk, then decide. This is the practical face of risk-based testing at the release gate.$L$, $L$A dashboard glowing '95 percent green' once reassured everyone into shipping, until someone noticed the red 5 percent was the tax calculation for the largest market. Always ask 'what is in the red?' before 'how much is green?' A single critical failure can outweigh a hundred cosmetic passes.$L$
