select c.id, $L$m11-15$L$, $L$Real World QA$L$, 11, $L$K3$L$, null, null, $L$A new payments feature reuses a shared checkout library. The change is small, but the library is used across many flows. How should regression testing be scoped in an ISTQB-aligned way?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Run targeted regression across the flows that depend on the shared library, guided by impact analysis and risk","correct":true,"why":"Change-related regression testing should cover areas affected by the change; impact analysis identifies which dependent flows carry the most risk and need re-testing."},{"text":"Re-run the entire test suite for every flow in the product regardless of dependency","correct":false,"why":"Full re-runs of unaffected areas waste time; regression should be scoped by what the change can affect and by risk."},{"text":"Only re-test the exact line changed and nothing else","correct":false,"why":"A shared library change can ripple into many flows, so testing only the changed line misses side effects, which is what regression aims to catch."},{"text":"Skip regression because the code change was small","correct":false,"why":"Small changes to widely shared code are precisely where regressions hide; change size does not equal change risk."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Regression testing checks that a change has not adversely affected previously working functionality. Impact analysis determines which parts of the system the change could affect, so regression can be scoped to dependent, higher-risk areas rather than either everything or nothing. This balances confidence against effort, which matters when suites are large and time is short.$L$, $L$A one-line tweak to a shared currency-formatting helper broke every invoice, receipt, and export in the product, none of which the author thought to test. A dependency map turned a scary 'test everything' into a focused list of the flows that actually consume that helper. Small diff, wide blast radius: regression follows dependencies, not line counts.$L$
