select c.id, $L$m11-16$L$, $L$Real World QA$L$, 11, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$A junior tester logs a defect as: 'Login broken. Doesn't work.' The developer bounces it back as not reproducible. Which ISTQB-aligned improvement to defect reporting best addresses this?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Provide clear steps to reproduce, expected vs actual results, environment, and severity/priority","correct":true,"why":"A useful defect report includes reproduction steps, expected and actual behaviour, environment details, and severity/priority so it can be understood, reproduced, and prioritised."},{"text":"Escalate to the manager so the developer is forced to accept it","correct":false,"why":"Escalation does not supply the information the developer needs to reproduce and fix the defect."},{"text":"Re-log the same wording but mark it critical to get attention","correct":false,"why":"Inflating priority without evidence damages trust and still lacks the details needed to act."},{"text":"Attach only a screenshot with no text","correct":false,"why":"A screenshot alone rarely conveys steps, environment, or expected behaviour needed to reproduce the issue."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Effective defect management depends on reports that let others reproduce and assess the issue: a clear summary, precise steps to reproduce, expected versus actual results, the environment and build, and severity and priority. Vague reports waste cycles bouncing between tester and developer. Good reports shorten the fix loop and support objective triage.$L$, $L$Rewriting 'login broken' as 'On build 4.2, Chrome 120, enter valid email + wrong password, click Sign In: expected inline error, actual blank page and 500 in console' turned a rejected ticket into a fix within the hour. The report is the interface between finding a defect and getting it fixed; ambiguity there taxes everyone.$L$
