  (course_id, ext_key, syllabus_topic, module_number, k_level, mock, mock_order, question_text, options, explanation, workplace_example)
select c.id, $L$m11-17$L$, $L$Real World QA$L$, 11, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$The team has met its agreed exit criteria (risk coverage targets hit, no open high-severity defects, test tasks complete), but a nervous stakeholder wants 'just a few more days of testing to be safe.' What does the ISTQB test process say about deciding to stop?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Completion is judged against defined exit criteria and residual risk, not against an open-ended feeling of safety","correct":true,"why":"Test completion (the exit criteria) provides an objective, agreed basis for stopping; testing can never be exhaustive, so open-ended 'more to be safe' has no defined end."},{"text":"Keep testing indefinitely until the stakeholder feels comfortable","correct":false,"why":"Comfort is subjective and unbounded; exhaustive testing is impossible, so there is no natural end to 'more to be safe.'"},{"text":"Stop the moment the calendar date arrives, ignoring criteria","correct":false,"why":"A date alone, without checking exit criteria and residual risk, is not a sound completion decision."},{"text":"Continue only if new defects are found, otherwise stop immediately","correct":false,"why":"The stop decision is based on exit criteria and residual risk, not solely on whether the last run found a defect."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$The test process defines exit or completion criteria (for example coverage of identified risks, defect thresholds, and completion of planned test tasks) that provide an objective basis for deciding when testing is done. Because exhaustive testing is impossible, there is no state of certainty; the criteria and the residual-risk picture are what let you stop responsibly and communicate the decision.$L$, $L$When a stakeholder asks for 'a bit more testing to be safe,' the productive reply is 'here are the exit criteria we agreed and where we stand; adding days targets which specific risk?' Often there is no named risk, just anxiety. Exit criteria turn an emotional debate into a factual, decidable one.$L$
