  (course_id, ext_key, syllabus_topic, module_number, k_level, mock, mock_order, question_text, options, explanation, workplace_example)
select c.id, $L$m11-18$L$, $L$Real World QA$L$, 11, $L$K3$L$, null, null, $L$Analysis of last quarter's defects shows 70 percent came from two of the twelve modules: the reporting engine and the import parser. With limited time this release, how should this shape test planning?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Concentrate additional test effort on the reporting engine and import parser, applying the defect-clustering insight","correct":true,"why":"Defects tend to cluster; a small number of modules often contain most defects, so focusing effort there is an efficient, risk-based use of limited time."},{"text":"Spread effort evenly across all twelve modules to be fair","correct":false,"why":"Even spreading ignores the evidence that risk is concentrated and dilutes effort away from the modules most likely to fail."},{"text":"Ignore those two modules since they were already tested last quarter","correct":false,"why":"Past testing does not immunise a defect-prone module; clustering suggests it will likely keep producing defects."},{"text":"Test only the modules with zero past defects to confirm they stay clean","correct":false,"why":"This inverts the risk, spending scarce time where defects are least likely and starving the high-risk modules."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$The defect-clustering principle observes that a small number of modules usually contain most of the defects found before release, or are responsible for most operational failures. Using historical defect data to focus testing on those clusters is a practical application of risk-based testing and makes limited effort go furthest. Pair it with the pesticide paradox by varying the tests you run there.$L$, $L$A defect heat-map showed the import parser lit up red quarter after quarter while eight modules stayed cold. Doubling down on the parser (with fresh test data, not the same old cases) caught three more bugs before release. Past defect density is one of the best predictors of where the next defect lives.$L$
