insert into public.practice_questions
  (course_id, ext_key, syllabus_topic, module_number, k_level, mock, mock_order, question_text, options, explanation, workplace_example)
select c.id, $L$m11-19$L$, $L$Real World QA$L$, 11, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$A developer argues that automated checks give '100 percent confidence' because code coverage is 95 percent. As a tester, which ISTQB point most accurately tempers this claim?$L$, $J$[{"text":"High coverage measures which code was exercised, not whether the tests assert correct behaviour or find all defect types","correct":true,"why":"Coverage shows structural exercise of code; it does not guarantee meaningful assertions, correct expected results, or that non-functional and requirement defects are caught, so it cannot equal 100 percent confidence."},{"text":"Coverage of 95 percent proves the software is defect-free","correct":false,"why":"This again violates the absence-of-defects principle and overstates what a coverage number means."},{"text":"Coverage is meaningless and should be ignored entirely","correct":false,"why":"Coverage is a useful metric for gaps; the point is not to over-interpret it, not to discard it."},{"text":"Only manual testing provides any real confidence","correct":false,"why":"Automated tests do provide confidence; the issue is interpreting coverage as total assurance."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Coverage metrics tell you what proportion of a coverage item (statements, branches) was exercised, which is valuable for finding untested gaps. But exercising code is not the same as verifying it behaves correctly, and coverage says nothing about missing requirements, usability, or performance. High coverage plus weak assertions can pass while defects remain. It informs confidence; it does not equal it.$L$, $L$A test that calls every line but asserts nothing beyond 'no exception thrown' can hit 95 percent coverage and still miss that the total is calculated wrong. Coverage answers 'did we run this code?' not 'did we check it does the right thing?' Treat the number as a gap-finder, not a guarantee.$L$
