  (course_id, ext_key, syllabus_topic, module_number, k_level, mock, mock_order, question_text, options, explanation, workplace_example)
select c.id, $L$m11-20$L$, $L$Real World QA$L$, 11, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$A tester relies on gut feel to probe a new API, quickly finding crashes on empty payloads and huge inputs that the scripted tests missed. Which ISTQB technique is being used, and how should it fit the overall approach?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Experience-based testing (such as error guessing and exploratory testing), used to complement, not replace, systematic techniques","correct":true,"why":"Experience-based techniques draw on the tester's knowledge to find defects that systematic cases miss; they add value alongside specification-based techniques rather than substituting for them."},{"text":"Decision table testing, since the tester considered combinations","correct":false,"why":"The tester used intuition and experience, not a structured enumeration of condition combinations."},{"text":"This is not a real technique and should not be counted as testing","correct":false,"why":"Experience-based testing is a recognised category in the syllabus with genuine defect-finding value."},{"text":"Boundary value analysis, because empty and huge inputs are boundaries","correct":false,"why":"BVA is a systematic specification-based technique; here the tester relied on experience and guessing rather than deriving boundaries from a spec."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Experience-based techniques, including error guessing and exploratory testing, use the tester's skill, intuition, and knowledge of likely failure points to design and run tests, often uncovering defects that formal techniques overlook. They are strongest as a complement to black-box and white-box techniques, adding adaptive, human insight on top of systematic coverage.$L$, $L$An experienced tester 'just knows' to send an emoji into a name field, paste 10,000 characters, or double-click submit, and these hunches catch defects no acceptance criterion listed. Pair that instinct with systematic boundary and decision-table cases and you get both breadth and depth.$L$
