select c.id, $L$m11-21$L$, $L$Real World QA$L$, 11, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$Your team practises testing throughout the sprint rather than in a phase at the end, and testers review stories during refinement. A new manager calls this 'wasteful' and wants a single test phase before each release. Which ISTQB idea best defends the current approach?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Early and continuous testing lowers the cost of defects by catching them closer to their introduction","correct":true,"why":"The early testing (shift-left) principle holds that starting test activities early, including static reviews, reduces rework because defects cost more the later they are found."},{"text":"Testing at the end is always cheaper because it happens once","correct":false,"why":"A single late phase concentrates defect discovery when fixes are most expensive, contradicting the early testing principle."},{"text":"Continuous testing removes the need for any release testing","correct":false,"why":"Early testing does not eliminate later test activities; it complements them by reducing the defects that reach them."},{"text":"The timing of testing makes no difference to defect cost","correct":false,"why":"The cost of fixing a defect rises the later it is found, so timing matters significantly."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$The principle of early testing states that test activities should start as early as possible in the lifecycle and continue throughout, because the cost of finding and fixing defects grows the later they are discovered. Reviewing stories in refinement and testing continuously catches defects near their source. A single end phase batches discovery into the most expensive window.$L$, $L$One team measured it: bugs caught in refinement cost minutes, bugs caught in the end-phase cost days of rework and re-testing, and bugs caught in production cost a hotfix plus a customer apology. 'Test at the end' is not one cheap phase; it is a pile of expensive late fixes wearing a trench coat.$L$
