insert into public.practice_questions
  (course_id, ext_key, syllabus_topic, module_number, k_level, mock, mock_order, question_text, options, explanation, workplace_example)
select c.id, $L$m11-22$L$, $L$Real World QA$L$, 11, $L$K3$L$, null, null, $L$A stakeholder says: 'We tested this thoroughly last release and it was fine, so we can reuse those exact results and skip testing the changed areas this time.' Which combination of ISTQB principles best explains why this is unsafe?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Testing is context dependent and defects can be introduced by changes, so prior results do not cover new or modified behaviour","correct":true,"why":"Testing depends on context (including the current build and changes), and any change can introduce regressions; last release's results say nothing about this release's changes."},{"text":"Exhaustive testing is possible, so last time's tests already covered everything","correct":false,"why":"Exhaustive testing is impossible, and even if prior testing were thorough it did not exercise the new changes."},{"text":"The pesticide paradox means old results are always still valid","correct":false,"why":"The paradox says repeating the same tests finds fewer new defects; it does not validate reusing old results for changed code."},{"text":"Testing shows the absence of defects, so a clean past result proves current safety","correct":false,"why":"This misstates the principle; testing shows presence, not absence, of defects, and cannot certify unchanged safety across a new build."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Testing is context dependent: results are tied to a specific build, configuration, and set of risks. When code changes, regression risk is introduced, so previously passing results do not certify the modified system. The safe response is change-based regression testing scoped by impact analysis. Reusing old results wholesale ignores both the context principle and the reality of regression.$L$, $L$A team once shipped by re-attaching last quarter's green test report, having 'only refactored the database layer.' The refactor broke sorting on every list in the app. Test evidence has a shelf life measured in builds; a change resets the clock on the areas it touches.$L$
