select c.id, $L$m11-23$L$, $L$Real World QA$L$, 11, $L$K3$L$, null, null, $L$You must choose a technique to test a discount rule: orders over 500 get 10 percent off, over 1000 get 20 percent off, and loyalty members get an extra 5 percent, with the tiers not stacking beyond the highest. Which approach gives the most reliable coverage?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Decision table testing for the discount combinations, plus boundary value analysis at 500 and 1000","correct":true,"why":"The outcome depends on combinations of conditions (amount tier and membership), suiting a decision table, while the tier thresholds are boundaries where off-by-one defects appear."},{"text":"Boundary value analysis alone at 500 and 1000","correct":false,"why":"BVA covers the thresholds but not the combinations with loyalty membership and the non-stacking rule."},{"text":"State transition testing of the discount states","correct":false,"why":"There is no sequence of states over time here, just conditions evaluated to select a discount."},{"text":"Error guessing based on past discount bugs","correct":false,"why":"Error guessing may help but cannot systematically cover the rule combinations and boundary thresholds on its own."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$When behaviour depends on combinations of conditions, a decision table captures each condition set and its expected discount, exposing the non-stacking rule and any missing combination. Boundary value analysis then verifies the exact tier thresholds (499/500, 1000/1001) where comparison-operator defects cluster. Combining specification-based techniques covers both the logic and the edges, which either alone would miss.$L$, $L$A promotions engine once stacked a 20 percent tier with a 20 percent coupon to give 40 percent off, refunding money the business never intended, because nobody tabulated the 'do these combine?' rules. A decision table forces those questions before launch, and boundary tests catch the 'over 1000' that quietly excluded exactly 1000.$L$
