  (course_id, ext_key, syllabus_topic, module_number, k_level, mock, mock_order, question_text, options, explanation, workplace_example)
select c.id, $L$m11-24$L$, $L$Real World QA$L$, 11, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$A tester says: 'I found zero defects in this feature, so my testing was low value this sprint.' Which ISTQB-aligned reframing is most accurate?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Finding no defects is a valid result; testing also builds confidence and evidence, provided the tests were well designed for the risks","correct":true,"why":"Testing has multiple objectives including gaining confidence and providing information for decisions; a well-designed test run that finds nothing still reduces uncertainty about risk."},{"text":"Zero defects proves the feature is defect-free and testing succeeded fully","correct":false,"why":"This overstates the result; testing shows presence, not absence, of defects, so zero found does not equal zero present."},{"text":"Testing that finds no defects was wasted and should not be reported","correct":false,"why":"It still provides information and confidence about the tested risks, which is a legitimate objective of testing."},{"text":"The tester must have missed defects, so the run is invalid","correct":false,"why":"No defects found does not automatically mean poor testing; it depends on whether the tests targeted the relevant risks."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Testing objectives include finding defects but also evaluating quality, building confidence, and providing information to support decisions. A well-designed test session that exercises the real risks and finds nothing genuinely lowers uncertainty. The caveat is the design: if the tests were weak, 'zero found' is uninformative. Value comes from covering the risks, not from a body count of defects.$L$, $L$Two testers report 'zero defects': one ran three trivial happy-path clicks, the other ran boundary, negative, and error-path cases across the risky flows. Same defect count, very different value. Judge a test run by the risk it covered, not only by the bugs it happened to find.$L$
