insert into public.practice_questions
  (course_id, ext_key, syllabus_topic, module_number, k_level, mock, mock_order, question_text, options, explanation, workplace_example)
select c.id, $L$m11-25$L$, $L$Real World QA$L$, 11, $L$K2$L$, null, null, $L$A team wants to buy a test automation tool. A vendor demo dazzles everyone, and a manager wants to purchase immediately and automate the whole suite next week. What does the ISTQB guidance on introducing a tool recommend?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Run a proof of concept or pilot to evaluate fit against real needs before rolling out, and account for maintenance and skills","correct":true,"why":"The syllabus advises assessing organisational maturity and needs, piloting the tool to evaluate it against reality, and recognising ongoing costs such as maintenance and training, rather than buying on a demo."},{"text":"Buy immediately, because a polished vendor demo proves the tool will work in your context","correct":false,"why":"A demo runs in an ideal setting; tool success is context dependent, so a pilot against your own needs is required."},{"text":"Automate the entire suite in the first week to maximise return","correct":false,"why":"A big-bang rollout ignores the learning curve, maintenance burden, and the need to prove value incrementally."},{"text":"Choose whichever tool is cheapest, since all automation tools are equivalent","correct":false,"why":"Tools differ in fit, integration, and maintainability; price alone ignores whether the tool meets the actual need."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Introducing a tool into an organisation is a project in itself. The syllabus recommends assessing the organisation's maturity and needs, evaluating the tool against those needs (often via a proof of concept or pilot), planning for the learning curve, and budgeting for ongoing maintenance and support. Success factors are about fit and adoption, not the polish of a sales demo.$L$, $L$A company bought a flashy record-and-playback tool on the strength of a demo; six months later the brittle scripts cost more to maintain than the manual tests they replaced, and it was shelved. A two-week pilot on a real, representative flow would have exposed that before the purchase order. Pilot first, buy second.$L$
