  (course_id, ext_key, syllabus_topic, module_number, k_level, mock, mock_order, question_text, options, explanation, workplace_example)
select c.id, $L$mock1-02$L$, $L$Fundamentals of Testing$L$, 1, $L$K2$L$, 1, 2, $L$A team believes that once testing is finished the software will be guaranteed defect-free. Why is this belief incorrect?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Testing can show the presence of defects but cannot prove their absence","correct":true,"why":"This is one of the seven testing principles: exhaustive testing is impossible, so passing tests reduce risk but cannot guarantee a defect-free product."},{"text":"Testing always finds every defect if enough test cases are written","correct":false,"why":"This contradicts the exhaustive-testing-is-impossible principle; you cannot test all inputs and paths."},{"text":"Defects are only introduced during maintenance, not during testing","correct":false,"why":"Defects can be introduced at any stage; this statement misstates when defects arise and does not explain the guarantee misconception."},{"text":"Debugging removes all remaining defects automatically after testing","correct":false,"why":"Debugging only addresses defects that have already been found and localized, and it can introduce new defects."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$The principle Tests show the presence of defects, not their absence means testing reduces the probability of undiscovered defects but never proves that none remain. Combined with the impossibility of exhaustive testing, no amount of passing tests guarantees a defect-free product.$L$, $L$A banking API passes its full regression suite before release, yet a rare currency-rounding defect surfaces in production, illustrating that passing tests did not prove absence of defects.$L$
