select c.id, $L$mock1-05$L$, $L$Fundamentals of Testing$L$, 1, $L$K1$L$, 1, 5, $L$In the sequence that leads to a failure, which order of concepts is correct?$L$, $J$[{"text":"A person makes an error, which introduces a defect, which may cause a failure","correct":true,"why":"This is the exact error-defect-failure chain defined in the syllabus."},{"text":"A failure introduces a defect, which causes an error","correct":false,"why":"This reverses the causal chain; failures are the observable result, not the origin."},{"text":"A defect creates an error, which produces a failure by itself","correct":false,"why":"An error is the human mistake that precedes the defect, not something a defect creates."},{"text":"A root cause is the same thing as a failure","correct":false,"why":"A root cause is the fundamental reason a defect was introduced, which is different from an observable failure."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$The syllabus defines an error (mistake) as a human action that produces an incorrect result, a defect (fault or bug) as the flaw introduced in the work product, and a failure as the observable incorrect behavior when the defect is executed. Not every defect leads to a failure.$L$, $L$A developer misreads a spec (error) and codes a discount as 15 percent instead of 50 percent (defect), which a customer sees as an overcharge at checkout (failure).$L$
