select c.id, $L$mock1-07$L$, $L$Software Development Lifecycle$L$, 2, $L$K1$L$, 1, 7, $L$In a sequential development model such as Waterfall, when does the majority of formal test execution typically take place?$L$, $J$[{"text":"After development is largely complete, in a dedicated later phase","correct":true,"why":"Sequential models complete phases one after another, so most dynamic testing is concentrated late, after the code is built."},{"text":"Continuously, in short overlapping cycles alongside every increment","correct":false,"why":"That describes iterative or incremental delivery, not a strict sequential Waterfall model."},{"text":"Only during the requirements phase, before any code exists","correct":false,"why":"Reviews of requirements happen early, but dynamic test execution needs a test object, so most execution is later."},{"text":"It is optional and usually skipped entirely in Waterfall","correct":false,"why":"Testing is a defined phase in Waterfall; it is not skipped, merely positioned late."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$In sequential SDLC models like Waterfall, phases follow one another and dynamic test execution is concentrated in a distinct testing phase after coding. This is a defining characteristic of how the chosen SDLC model shapes the timing of test activities.$L$, $L$A banking core-system project using Waterfall builds all modules first, then runs a three-month system test phase before go-live, finding many integration defects late.$L$
