select c.id, $L$mock1-09$L$, $L$Software Development Lifecycle$L$, 2, $L$K2$L$, 1, 9, $L$Which statement best distinguishes iterative development from incremental development?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Iterative refines the product through repeated cycles; incremental delivers the product in separately usable pieces","correct":true,"why":"Iterative development revisits and refines functionality over cycles, while incremental development adds and delivers functionality piece by piece."},{"text":"Iterative has no testing; incremental tests only at the end","correct":false,"why":"Both approaches include testing throughout; neither omits it."},{"text":"They are identical terms with no practical difference","correct":false,"why":"They are related but distinct: one emphasizes refinement, the other emphasizes staged delivery of pieces."},{"text":"Incremental is only used in Waterfall projects","correct":false,"why":"Incremental delivery is characteristic of Agile and other non-sequential models, not Waterfall."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Iterative models develop the system through cycles that refine features, whereas incremental models build and deliver the system in increments, each adding usable functionality. Understanding this distinction is part of recognizing how different SDLC models organize development and testing.$L$, $L$A retail app team ships a basic checkout increment first, then in later iterations refines the same checkout with saved cards and coupon logic based on user feedback.$L$
