select c.id, $L$mock1-10$L$, $L$Software Development Lifecycle$L$, 2, $L$K2$L$, 1, 10, $L$How does an iterative, incremental SDLC most directly affect testing compared with a sequential model?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Regression testing becomes more frequent because each iteration can affect previously working features","correct":true,"why":"Repeated integration of new increments risks breaking existing behavior, so regression testing must run often."},{"text":"Test levels are no longer needed","correct":false,"why":"Test levels still apply; they are simply exercised within each iteration."},{"text":"Regression testing becomes unnecessary because features are small","correct":false,"why":"Smaller changes still risk regressions; frequent change actually increases regression need."},{"text":"Only manual testing can be used","correct":false,"why":"Frequent iterations typically increase reliance on automation, not manual-only testing."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Because iterative and incremental models integrate change repeatedly, each iteration can disturb previously validated functionality, making frequent regression testing essential. This is a clear example of how the chosen SDLC influences testing effort and scope.$L$, $L$A SaaS team runs its full automated regression suite every sprint, because adding a new reporting feature last month accidentally broke an existing export function.$L$
