select c.id, $L$mock1-23$L$, $L$Test Analysis & Design Techniques$L$, 4, $L$K3$L$, 1, 23, $L$A withdrawal amount must be between 10 and 500 inclusive. Using the 3-value boundary approach, how many distinct boundary test values are there in total?$L$, $J$[{"text":"6.","correct":true,"why":"Lower boundary gives 9, 10, 11 and upper gives 499, 500, 501: six distinct values."},{"text":"4.","correct":false,"why":"Four would be the 2-value approach (9, 10, 499, 500... actually 9, 10, 500, 501)."},{"text":"3.","correct":false,"why":"Three values cover only one boundary, not both."},{"text":"8.","correct":false,"why":"There are only two boundaries, each contributing three values, giving six, not eight."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$The 3-value approach tests each boundary and both neighbours. Lower boundary 10 gives 9, 10, 11; upper boundary 500 gives 499, 500, 501. Six distinct values because the two boundaries do not overlap.$L$, $L$An ATM tester lists exactly six amounts (9, 10, 11, 499, 500, 501) as the boundary cases for the withdrawal limit, giving a tidy, defensible boundary matrix in the test design.$L$
