select c.id, $L$mock1-38$L$, $L$Test Tools$L$, 9, $L$K1$L$, 1, 38, $L$What is the primary purpose of test tools in the software testing process?$L$, $J$[{"text":"To support and improve test activities, making them more efficient or enabling activities that would be hard to do manually","correct":true,"why":"ISTQB describes tool support as improving the efficiency and effectiveness of testing and enabling activities (such as large-scale regression) that are impractical by hand."},{"text":"To remove the need for testers by fully replacing human judgement","correct":false,"why":"Tools support testers; they do not replace the human skill, judgement and analysis that testing requires."},{"text":"To guarantee that the software is completely free of defects","correct":false,"why":"No tool can guarantee defect-free software; testing shows the presence of defects, not their absence."},{"text":"To automatically write the requirements and design the system","correct":false,"why":"Test tools support testing activities, not the authoring of product requirements or system design."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Test tools exist to support test activities. They can make work more efficient (for example automating repetitive checks), improve consistency and repeatability, and enable activities such as high-volume performance testing that are impractical manually. They complement, not replace, tester skill.$L$, $L$A team spends two days each release running the same 300 regression checks by hand. Introducing an execution tool lets those checks run overnight, freeing testers to explore new features - a classic case of a tool supporting, not replacing, the testers.$L$
