select c.id, $L$mock1-40$L$, $L$Agile Testing$L$, 10, $L$K1$L$, 1, 40, $L$In agile software development, how often is working software typically expected to be delivered?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Frequently, in short iterations of a few weeks or less","correct":true,"why":"Agile favours frequent delivery of working software over short timescales, which shapes how and when testing happens."},{"text":"Only once, at the very end of the project","correct":false,"why":"That describes a sequential (waterfall) delivery, not an agile approach."},{"text":"Whenever the full set of requirements is finally signed off","correct":false,"why":"Agile does not wait for a complete, frozen requirement set before delivering value."},{"text":"Every few years as part of a major release cycle","correct":false,"why":"Multi-year cycles are the opposite of the short, frequent iterations agile promotes."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$A core agile principle is the frequent delivery of working software in short iterations. This compresses the time available for testing each increment and pushes teams toward continuous, integrated testing rather than a separate end-of-project test phase.$L$, $L$A team delivers a potentially shippable increment every two-week sprint, so testers plan, execute and report on tests within each sprint rather than saving verification for a final test stage.$L$
