select c.id, $L$mock2-05$L$, $L$Fundamentals of Testing$L$, 1, $L$K2$L$, 2, 5, $L$A regression suite that once found many defects now rarely finds any, even though the product still has bugs. Which principle explains this?$L$, $J$[{"text":"The pesticide paradox: repeating the same tests stops finding new defects","correct":true,"why":"The pesticide paradox states that reusing identical tests loses effectiveness over time, so tests must be reviewed and updated."},{"text":"Defects cluster together in a few modules","correct":false,"why":"Defect clustering explains where defects concentrate, not why repeated tests stop finding them."},{"text":"Testing is context dependent","correct":false,"why":"Context dependence explains that approaches differ by situation, not the declining yield of unchanged tests."},{"text":"Early testing saves time and money","correct":false,"why":"Early testing addresses when to test, not the diminishing returns of repeated identical tests."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$The pesticide paradox principle says that running the same tests repeatedly eventually stops revealing new defects, so tests need to be regularly reviewed, revised, and augmented with new ones. This keeps the suite effective as the product evolves.$L$, $L$A team refreshes its e-commerce regression tests each quarter, adding scenarios for new promo-code rules, because the old fixed tests had stopped catching defects.$L$
