select c.id, $L$mock2-10$L$, $L$Software Development Lifecycle$L$, 2, $L$K2$L$, 2, 10, $L$In Test-Driven Development (TDD), what is the correct order of the core cycle?$L$, $J$[{"text":"Write a failing test, write just enough code to pass it, then refactor","correct":true,"why":"TDD follows red-green-refactor: a failing test first, minimal code to pass, then cleanup."},{"text":"Write all production code, then write tests to match it","correct":false,"why":"That is test-after development, the opposite of TDD's test-first approach."},{"text":"Write documentation, then code, then delete the tests","correct":false,"why":"TDD keeps its tests as a living regression safety net; it does not delete them."},{"text":"Write acceptance tests only, with no unit tests","correct":false,"why":"That resembles ATDD; TDD is driven by fine-grained developer tests."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$TDD is a test-first approach where a failing test is written before production code, enough code is added to make it pass, and the design is then refactored. It is one of the approaches in which testing drives development, as covered in the SDLC syllabus area.$L$, $L$A developer building a discount calculator writes a failing unit test for a 10 percent rule, codes just enough to pass it, then cleans up the method before adding the next rule.$L$
