select c.id, $L$mock2-14$L$, $L$Static Testing$L$, 3, $L$K2$L$, 2, 14, $L$How are static and dynamic testing BEST characterized in relation to each other?$L$, $J$[{"text":"They are complementary, finding different types of defects and together improving quality","correct":true,"why":"Each targets defects the other tends to miss, so using both gives the broadest coverage."},{"text":"Dynamic testing makes static testing unnecessary","correct":false,"why":"Dynamic testing cannot find requirements ambiguities or dead code as effectively as static testing."},{"text":"They find exactly the same defects, so only one is needed","correct":false,"why":"They find different kinds of defects, so they are not interchangeable."},{"text":"Static testing must always be performed after dynamic testing","correct":false,"why":"Static testing is typically done earlier, often before any code is executable."}]$J$::jsonb, $L$Static and dynamic testing share the goal of supporting quality but are complementary. Static testing finds defects directly in work products; dynamic testing observes failures caused by defects during execution.$L$, $L$A team reviews requirements statically to remove ambiguity, then runs functional tests dynamically to confirm the built feature behaves as agreed.$L$
